## 1 Introduction

The financial services sector is central to Hong Kong’s economy, employing over 270,000 professionals and contributing 23% of GDP [[7](https://arxiv.org/html/2509.08001v1#bib.bib7)]. Yet beyond macroeconomic indicators, the micro-dynamics of professional mobility remain poorly understood. Employee turnover in particular represents both a systemic risk and a major operational cost, with annual rates exceeding 24%.11 1 [FSDC Report (2023)](https://www.fsdc.org.hk/media/52ulbcmf/20230824_-en-nurturing-the-young-talents-of-today-for-hk-s-fs-industry-of-tomorrow_fv.pdf)

Recent work has begun to map Hong Kong’s financial ecosystem through the Securities and Futures Commission (SFC) register, revealing its large-scale network of professionals and firms [[1](https://arxiv.org/html/2509.08001v1#bib.bib1)]. In this paper, we move from mapping to forecasting: can we predict when a professional will leave their firm, and does the surrounding network structure improve predictive power?

To address this, we construct temporal networks from 2007–2024, capturing career trajectories of 121,883 professionals across 4,979 firms. We develop a graph-based feature propagation framework to quantify peer influence and organizational stability, and integrate these signals into predictive models. Our approach tackles four challenges related to workforce analytics:

1. 1. Network contagion: capturing peer turnover cascades through temporal propagation; 2. 2. Temporal dynamics: evaluating models with strict walk-forward validation; 3. 3. Class imbalance: rare-event prediction with only 2.3% monthly turnover; 4. 4. Feature engineering: combining individual, organizational, and propagated network features.

Network contagion: capturing peer turnover cascades through temporal propagation;

Temporal dynamics: evaluating models with strict walk-forward validation;

Class imbalance: rare-event prediction with only 2.3% monthly turnover;

Feature engineering: combining individual, organizational, and propagated network features.

We show that network-derived signals substantially improve predictive accuracy over non-network baselines, and that firm-level stability and peer influence dominate demographic attributes. These findings highlight the role of contagion in financial labor markets and open the door to network-based workforce analytics with applications in recruiting, talent management, systemic risk assessment, and even the design of immigration policies for skilled workers.
